WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:10.620
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. OK, here is something that we did not expect. Check this out. This was recorded on a stage in Brooklyn, St. Francis College.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。好的，这里有一件我们意想不到的事情。看看这个。这是在布鲁克林圣弗朗西斯学院的舞台上录制的。

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.970
<v Actor>To be or not to be, that is the question.
<v Actor>生存还是毁灭，这是一个值得考虑的问题。

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:20.510
<v Ira Glass>Same month in Hawaii, on the island of Oahu.
<v Ira Glass>同一个月在夏威夷，瓦胡岛上。

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:37.480
<v Actor>To be or not to be, that is the question-- whether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer the slings and arrows of outrageous fortune or to take arms against a sea of troubles and by opposing, end them.
<v Actor>生存还是毁灭，这是一个值得考虑的问题——究竟哪种更高贵：是忍受狂暴命运的毒箭，还是挺身反抗无边的苦难，通过斗争结束它们。

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.280
<v Ira Glass>Same month, a professional company in Boston, Massachusetts, at the Public Theater.
<v Ira Glass>同一个月，马萨诸塞州波士顿的一家专业剧团，在公共剧院。

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:59.800
<v Actor>Whether 'tis nobler in the mind to suffer the slings and arrows of outrageous fortune or to take arms against a sea of troubles and by opposing, end them.
<v Actor>究竟哪种更高贵：是忍受狂暴命运的毒箭，还是挺身反抗无边的苦难，通过斗争结束它们。

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.240
<v Ira Glass>In Colorado, at Camp Shakespeare, you can actually hear the sneakers of the teenaged Hamlet squeaking.
<v Ira Glass>在科罗拉多州的莎士比亚训练营，你甚至能听到十几岁的哈姆雷特扮演者运动鞋发出的吱吱声。

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.850
<v Ira Glass>[SNEAKERS SQUEAKING]
<v Ira Glass>[运动鞋摩擦声]

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.510
<v Actor>Why do you go about to recover the wind of me?
<v Actor>你为什么要试图窥探我的心思？

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.490
<v Actor>Oh my lord, if my duty be too bold--
<v Actor>哦，大人，如果我的忠诚过于唐突——

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:27.520
<v Ira Glass>And at the Bay Area Shakespeare Camp, presented by the San Francisco Shakespeare Festival, the kids put on a production that they have concocted that is made up entirely of death scenes from different Shakespeare plays. That seems because, um, kids love death scenes.
<v Ira Glass>在旧金山莎士比亚节举办的湾区莎士比亚夏令营里，孩子们自编自导了一出戏，完全由莎士比亚不同剧作中的死亡场景组成。这似乎是因为，嗯，孩子们喜欢死亡场景。

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:34.930
<v Actor>No, no! The drink, the drink! O, my dear Hamlet, the drink, the drink! I am poisoned! [GAGGING]
<v Actor>不，不！那杯酒，那杯酒！哦，亲爱的哈姆雷特，那杯酒，那杯酒！我中毒了！[呕吐声]

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:35.420
<v Actor>[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]
<v Actor>[观众笑声]

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:43.940
<v Ira Glass>Eight-year-old Marissa Graham grabs her throat like a cat in a violent cartoon. Everyone in the production is eight, except for Polonius, who's six.
<v Ira Glass>八岁的玛丽莎·格雷厄姆像暴力卡通片里的猫一样抓住喉咙。剧组里每个人都是八岁，除了饰演波洛尼厄斯的那个孩子，他只有六岁。

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.930
<v Actor>The rest is silence.
<v Actor>余下的只有沉默。

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:55.000
<v Actor>[AUDIENCE CHUCKLING, APPLAUDING]
<v Actor>[观众笑声、掌声]

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:16.340
<v Ira Glass>There's actually no way to tell how many productions of Hamlet are up in any given month. In summer 2002, when we first broadcast today's show and when we made these recordings, the American Theatre website listed 12 theaters doing Shakespeare's Hamlet, 11 productions of the play I Hate Hamlet, one production of something called Hamlet Dreams, and one theater doing the dreadful and feared Hamletmachine.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，无法统计在任何一个月里有多少场《哈姆雷特》正在上演。2002年夏天，当我们首次播出今天的节目并进行这些录音时，美国戏剧网站列出了12家正在排演莎士比亚《哈姆雷特》的剧院，11场名为《我恨哈姆雷特》的演出，一场名为《哈姆雷特之梦》的演出，还有一家剧院正在排演那部可怕而令人恐惧的《哈姆雷特机器》。

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:39.670
<v Ira Glass>Of course, Hamlet itself is kind of a weird play. The central character is in a situation that very few of us are ever going to find ourselves in. His uncle killed his father and then married his mother in order to become the king. The play is four hours long. The main conflict of the play is a guy debating, in long, complicated monologues, whether or not he should kill somebody. What is there in that for most of us to relate to?
<v Ira Glass>当然，《哈姆雷特》本身是一部奇怪的剧。主角所处的境地是我们大多数人永远不会遇到的。他的叔叔杀了他父亲，然后娶了他母亲，只为了成为国王。这部剧长达四个小时。剧中的主要矛盾是一个男人在冗长而复杂的独白中纠结是否应该杀人。这对我们大多数人来说有什么共鸣之处呢？

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:50.860
<v Ira Glass>Unless, of course, we happen to be murderers. And what would the play be like if it were actually performed by murderers and other violent criminals? What would they see that the rest of us do not?
<v Ira Glass>除非我们刚好是杀人犯。如果这出戏真的由杀人犯和其他暴力罪犯来表演会是什么样？他们会看到什么我们看不到的东西呢？

00:02:50.860 --> 00:03:24.730
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program we answer that question. And the answer is, a lot. Over the course of six months, reporter Jack Hitt visited prisoners at the Missouri Eastern Correctional Center-- which is a high-security prison-- from their first rehearsal to their last performance of Act V of Shakespeare's Hamlet. We're devoting our entire program today to the drama that he found there. Our program today, "Act V." We bring you what we believe is one of the most evocative productions of Shakespeare done anywhere in 2002, one you would have had a hard time getting tickets to-- it was only performed in prison. Here's Jack Hitt.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，今天我们的节目将回答这个问题。答案是，有很多。在六个月的时间里，记者杰克·希特访问了密苏里州东部惩教中心的囚犯——这是一所高度安全监狱——从他们的第一次排练到最后演出莎士比亚《哈姆雷特》第五幕。我们今天将整期节目奉献给他在那里发现的戏剧。我们今天的节目名为“第五幕”。我们为您带来我们认为2002年任何地方演出的最令人回味的莎士比亚剧作之一，你可能很难买到票，因为它只在监狱里演出。下面是杰克·希特。

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:53.440
<v Jack Hitt>The first thing they hand me when I pass through the thick iron doors is a tiny black box called a screamer. Pull the cord attached, and a phalanx of armed guards will sweep from all points of the prison and try to rescue me. I keep it in my pocket as I enter a huge yard with more than 1,000 prisoners wandering around. Some of the guys are playing handball against walls, some are lifting weights as I walk across, most are friendly. A couple of the skinheads, their arms dense with spider tattoos, narrow their eyes as I pass.
<v Jack Hitt>当我穿过厚重的铁门时，他们给我的第一样东西是一个被称为“尖叫器”的小黑盒。拉动附带的绳子，一队武装警卫会从监狱的四面八方涌来试图营救我。我把它放在口袋里，走进了一个有超过1000名囚犯漫步其中的巨大庭院。有些人在对着墙打手球，有些人正在举重。当我走过时，大多数人很友好。几个手臂上布满蜘蛛纹身的光头党在我经过时眯起了眼睛。

00:03:53.440 --> 00:04:06.540
<v Jack Hitt>Across the yard, there's a big building. A long corridor leads to a door marked Education Annex. Inside, there's room D-168, where a small, white-haired woman, Agnes Wilcox, is holding auditions for Hamlet.
<v Jack Hitt>院子对面有一座大楼。一条长长的走廊通向一扇标有“教育附件”的门。里面是D-168室，一位留着白发的矮小女性艾格尼丝·威尔科克斯正在为《哈姆雷特》选角。

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:29.240
<v Agnes Wilcox>Let me see. If you're not Hamlet, Horatio, Laertes, Claudius, or Fortinbras, would you write down roles that interest you? Those roles are Clown 1 and 2, we've got Priest, we've got Osric.
<v Agnes Wilcox>让我看看。如果你不是哈姆雷特、霍拉旭、雷欧提斯、克劳狄斯或福丁布拉，请写下你感兴趣的角色。那些角色是小丑1和2，还有牧师，还有奥斯里克。

00:04:29.240 --> 00:05:12.860
<v Jack Hitt>Dressed in loose prison uniforms, the actors sit around tables in the cinder block classroom beneath inspirational slogans-- believe in yourself, think positively. The aspiring cast is half black, half white, and ranges from young lifers in their 20s to old timers in their 50s. Because it's against the rules to congregate an audience of felons for the four hours it would take to perform the whole play, Agnes has staged one act every six months, starting in December, 1999. Tonight is the first read through for Act V, the final bloody climax. Many of the inmates here never finished high school, and all they had known of Shakespeare was the phrase "To be or not to be." Tonight they are hearing some of the other famous speeches for the first time.
<v Jack Hitt>穿着宽松的囚服，演员们坐在 cinder block 教室的桌子旁，墙上挂着鼓舞人心的标语——相信自己，积极思考。这些有抱负的演员有一半是黑人，一半是白人，年龄从二十多岁的年轻无期徒刑犯到五十多岁的老资历都有。因为规定禁止为演出整部戏所需的四个小时聚集大批囚犯，艾格尼丝从1999年12月开始，每六个月排演一幕。今晚是第五幕的第一次剧本研读，这是最后的血腥高潮。这里的许多囚犯从未读完高中，他们对莎士比亚唯一的了解就是那句“生存还是毁灭”。今晚他们第一次听到了其他一些著名的演讲。

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:16.540
<v Actor>A pestilence on him, for a mad rouge.
<v Actor>对他这个疯子施加天谴。

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:17.360
<v Agnes Wilcox>Rogue.
<v Agnes Wilcox>无赖。

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.530
<v Actor>Rogue. He poured a flax--
<v Actor>无赖。他曾经把一瓶——

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:22.700
<v Agnes Wilcox>Flagon.
<v Agnes Wilcox>酒壶。

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.320
<v Actor>Flagon of--
<v Actor>一壶——

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:24.980
<v Agnes Wilcox>Rhenish.
<v Agnes Wilcox>莱茵酒。

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:37.030
<v Actor>Rhenish on my head once. This same skull, sir, was sure Yorick's skull, the king's jester.
<v Actor>莱茵酒泼在我的头上。先生，这个头骨，确实是约里克的头骨，也就是国王的弄臣。

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:46.370
<v Actor>Alas, poor Yorick. I knew him, Horatio. A fellow of infinite jest, of most excellent fancy. He hath bore me on his back 1,000 times--
<v Actor>唉，可怜的约里克。我认识他，霍拉旭。一个有着无穷诙谐和绝佳想象力的家伙。他曾一千次把我背在背上——

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:57.740
<v Jack Hitt>Most read-throughs with any cast are terrible. This one is terrible too. The actors know it. But they plow on through the script. An inmate named Paul, who serves as Agnes' assistant director, explains why.
<v Jack Hitt>大多数剧组的第一次剧本研读都很糟糕。这次也不例外。演员们自己也知道。但他们还是坚持读完了剧本。一位名叫保罗的囚犯，担任艾格尼丝的助理导演，解释了原因。

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:26.080
<v Paul>The first two or three acts I thought, oh no, there's no way. There's no way we're going to get this thing down and go and do a live performance and-- doing a soliloquy that's two pages long, I thought there's-- no, no. This is impossible. But what we're learning here, I guess, from performing it and hearing it done time again, was the "It'll be OK. It'll work out."
<v Paul>前两三幕时我想，哦不，不可能。我们不可能掌握这个并进行现场演出，还要表演两页长的独白，我想这——不，不。这太不可能了。但我想我们在这里从一遍又一遍的表演和聆听中学到的是，“没事的，会好起来的。”

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:49.540
<v Jack Hitt>The local critics agreed with Paul. The Act IV performance earned a review in the St. Louis paper. The actors were said to be commanding and compelling. With the entire performance, the reviewer wrote, "A 400-year-old text is restored to freshness." It seemed hard to imagine.
<v Jack Hitt>当地的评论家同意保罗的看法。第四幕的演出在圣路易斯的报纸上获得了评论。演员们被评价为气场强大且引人入胜。评论家写道，通过这次完整的演出，“一部400年前的文本焕发了新生。”这似乎很难想象。

00:06:49.540 --> 00:07:05.950
<v Jack Hitt>I've seen Hamlet a dozen times. I've seen Kevin Kline do it at the Public Theater. I saw the famous Diane Venora version three nights in a row. I even saw Ingmar Bergman's production done in Brooklyn, performed entirely in Swedish. What else is there to learn from watching another Hamlet?
<v Jack Hitt>我看过十几遍《哈姆雷特》。我见过凯文·克莱恩在公共剧院的演出。我连续三个晚上看过著名的戴安·薇诺拉版本。我甚至看过英格玛·伯格曼在布鲁克林制作、全瑞典语表演的版本。通过看另一场《哈姆雷特》还能学到什么呢？

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:32.180
<v Jack Hitt>But the truth is, this production was different. Because this is a play about a man pondering a violent crime and its consequences performed by violent criminals living out those consequences. After hanging out with this group of convicted actors for six months, I did discover something. I didn't know anything about Hamlet.
<v Jack Hitt>但事实是，这次演出与众不同。因为这是一部关于一个男人思考暴力犯罪及其后果的戏，由正在亲身经历这些后果的暴力罪犯表演。在与这群被定罪的演员相处了六个月后，我确实发现了一些事情。我以前对《哈姆雷特》一无所知。

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:51.530
<v Jack Hitt>Remember how it starts. Hamlet sees his father's ghost, who explains he was murdered by Claudius, Hamlet's uncle. The ghost commands Hamlet to avenge his death by killing Claudius. The play is basically Hamlet pondering this single horrific action, and whether he can be the man to do it. "To be or not to be."
<v Jack Hitt>记得它是怎么开始的吗？哈姆雷特见到了他父亲的鬼魂，鬼魂解释说他被叔叔克劳狄斯谋杀了。鬼魂命令哈姆雷特通过杀死克劳狄斯来复仇。这出戏本质上就是哈姆雷特在思考这个极其可怕的行为，以及他是否能成为那个动手的人。“生存还是毁灭。”

00:07:51.530 --> 00:08:05.150
<v Jack Hitt>These days, when people say the name Hamlet, it's usually just a metaphor, shorthand for somebody who is afraid to act, who dithers and thinks too much. We almost forget just what action Hamlet was contemplating. These actors haven't forgotten.
<v Jack Hitt>这些天，当人们提到“哈姆雷特”这个名字时，它通常只是一个隐喻，是那些不敢行动、犹豫不决、想得太多的人的缩写。我们几乎忘记了哈姆雷特究竟在考虑什么行动。而这些演员没有忘记。

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:16.580
<v Manuel Johnson>My name is Manuel Johnson, and I'm 36 years old. I'm here for two first degree assaults. I play Hamlet.
<v Manuel Johnson>我叫曼努埃尔·约翰逊，今年36岁。我因两项一级袭击罪入狱。我饰演哈姆雷特。

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:28.600
<v Jack Hitt>When you're onstage doing Hamlet, what do you draw upon in your own experience to make the character come to life?
<v Jack Hitt>当你在台上表演哈姆雷特时，你从自己的经历中汲取了什么来让这个角色栩栩如生？

00:08:28.600 --> 00:09:10.870
<v Manuel Johnson>The idea of wanting to hurt someone. I have experience hurting someone-- you know, to the point of their life may be in danger. I was a very confused and angry person. And you know, it escalated into, you know, me shooting two people and leaving them for dead.
<v Manuel Johnson>想要伤害别人的想法。我有伤害人的经历——你知道，到了生命可能处于危险的地步。我当时是一个非常困惑和愤怒的人。你知道，这升级为我射击了两个人，并把他们丢在那里等死。

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:18.450
<v Chris Harris>My name is Chris Harris. I'm 38 years old. And I play the fourth character of Hamlet.
<v Chris Harris>我叫克里斯·哈里斯。今年38岁。我扮演哈姆雷特的四个角色之一。

00:09:18.450 --> 00:10:00.790
<v Jack Hitt>Let me explain something. The character of Hamlet is played by four people. They are all on stage at the same time, taking turns delivering the lines. Agnes did this partly to give more actors speaking roles and not saddle any one man with all that dialogue. But as theatre, it works. Hamlet's role is full of long soliloquies and rhetorical asides. This small gang of Hamlets, which mutters to itself and laughs at its own jokes, nicely captures that fractured quality of Hamlet's different personalities. And it's also bonded the four actors together. They call themselves "The Hamlets" and constantly talk about their character. To Chris, it seems like Hamlet is just the fifth guy in their odd clique-- another criminal with a complicated past.
<v Jack Hitt>让我解释一下。哈姆雷特的角色由四个人共同担任。他们同时在台上，轮流说台词。艾格尼丝这样做部分是为了让更多的演员有发言权，而不让任何一个人负担所有的台词。但作为戏剧，它奏效了。哈姆雷特的角色充满了长篇独白和反讽的旁白。这一小群哈姆雷特，对自己嘀咕、嘲笑自己的笑话，恰如其分地捕捉到了哈姆雷特人格分裂的特质。这也将这四位演员紧密地联系在一起。他们称自己为“哈姆雷特们”，并不断讨论他们的角色。对克里斯来说，哈姆雷特就像他们这个奇怪小圈子里的第五个人——另一个有着复杂过去的罪犯。

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:35.180
<v Chris Harris>Let me put it in terms of the year 2000s. What we have is an upper-middle class youngster, 19, 20 years old, who comes from a well-to-do family. You know, they own quite a bit of land. The people in the small town respect him and love him. And his uncle murdered his father and is now married to his mother. So, I mean, all kinds of serious issues there, all kinds.
<v Chris Harris>让我用2000年代的说法来解释。我们拥有的是一个来自富有家庭、19、20岁的上中产阶级年轻人。你知道，他们拥有相当多的土地。小镇上的人尊重他、爱戴他。他的叔叔杀了他父亲，现在娶了他母亲。所以，我的意思是，这其中有各种严重的矛盾，各种各样。

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:43.970
<v Jack Hitt>Chris and the Hamlets practice alone most days. Finding time when the four can just get together by themselves is tough when there's always 1,000 inmates around. So they do it where they can.
<v Jack Hitt>克里斯和“哈姆雷特们”大多数日子里独自练习。当周围总有1000名囚犯时，找到四个人能聚在一起的时间很困难。所以他们在力所能及的地方排练。

00:10:43.970 --> 00:11:08.600
<v Chris Harris>On every Sunday after the noon meal, the four of us will assemble. We'll have our little gray Hamlet books, and we'll proceed down to the track. And if you were simply walking by, what you'd probably hear is-- you'd hear this chatter of somebody giving their lines clearly, the rest of us with our heads down in the books, walking the line.
<v Chris Harris>每个周日午餐后，我们四个人就会集合。我们拿着我们的小灰色《哈姆雷特》书，沿着轨道走。如果你只是路过，你可能会听到的是——你会听到有人清晰地背诵台词的喋喋不休，我们其余人低着头看书，沿着线走。

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:31.020
<v Chris Harris>Now, there are people-- because there are benches that are all throughout the inside of the track. So there are people that actually watch us. So you'll hear this old English-style speech. You know, "Ho, Horatio," and these people are like, "Ho, what"?
<v Chris Harris>现在，轨道内侧到处都是长凳。所以实际上有人在看我们。所以你会听到这种古英语风格的演讲。你知道，“嘿，霍拉旭，”而这些人会说，“嘿，什么？”

00:11:31.020 --> 00:12:11.210
<v Jack Hitt>One of the problems of doing any play in prison is that being a good actor is the exact emotional opposite of what it takes to be a successful inmate. Rather than close off all feeling and look tough, you have to open your vulnerable self up and withstand often cruel laughter as you try to find some authentic emotion within you. In this way, a level-four high-security prison is no different from high school. And so most of the inmates who audition for Agnes tend to be, you know, actor-y people-- the theatre-types of prison. Back in 1999, they just had to put up with abuse from the bigger, meaner inmates. But that changed. In fact, a lot changed, after Agnes cast the role of Hamlet's best friend.
<v Jack Hitt>在监狱里排演任何戏剧的一个问题是，做一个好演员与做一个成功的囚犯在情感上是截然相反的。与其封闭所有感觉、表现得强硬，你必须敞开脆弱的自我，忍受常常残酷的嘲笑，试图在内心找到某种真实的情感。从这个意义上讲，四级高度安全监狱与高中没什么不同。所以大多数报名参加艾格尼丝选角的囚犯往往是，你知道，那种有表演欲的人——监狱里的戏剧类型。早在1999年，他们还得忍受更强壮、更刻薄的囚犯的虐待。但情况改变了。事实上，在艾格尼丝选定哈姆雷特最好的朋友这个角色后，很多事情都改变了。

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:21.290
<v Big Hutch>My name is Derrick, Big Hutch. I just-- I play Horatio, the scholar.
<v Big Hutch>我叫德里克，“大哈奇”。我——我扮演霍拉旭，那个学者。

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:40.550
<v Jack Hitt>You might be surprised to learn that Derrick "Big Hutch" Hutchison is big. He has a smooth, bald skull and hooded threatening eyes-- the kind of guy that if you met him, you might think, he's probably serving 120 years for armed robberies. And that would be correct. Hutch isn't like the rest of the cast, and he's the first person to tell you.
<v Jack Hitt>你可能会惊讶地发现德里克·“大哈奇”·哈奇森个头很大。他有着光滑的秃顶和充满威胁感的深陷眼窝——那种如果你见到他，可能会认为他因为武装抢劫被判了120年刑的人。你是对的。哈奇和其他演员不一样，他是第一个告诉你这一点的人。

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:54.590
<v Big Hutch>In prison, you got this hierarchy system. Listen, let's compare it to the ocean. You've got the minnows, and you've got the killer whale. Minnow being the lowest, killer whale the highest.
<v Big Hutch>在监狱里，你有这种等级制度。听着，让我们把它比作海洋。你有小鱼，你有虎鲸。小鱼是最低级的，虎鲸是最高级的。

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.920
<v Jack Hitt>So, which are you, Big Hutch?
<v Jack Hitt>那么，你是哪种，大哈奇？

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:58.510
<v Big Hutch>I'm the blue whale.
<v Big Hutch>我是蓝鲸。

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:01.760
<v Jack Hitt>[CHUCKLING] Right.
<v Jack Hitt>[笑声]对。

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:18.350
<v Big Hutch>That mean I control the killer whales, and I can eat up the minnows if I want. And I mean, that's how it is. Most of the guys in Hamlet, they're minnows. I mean, I don't normally would associate with them.
<v Big Hutch>这意味着我控制虎鲸，如果我想，我可以吃掉小鱼。我的意思是，事实就是这样。哈姆雷特剧组里的大多数人，他们是小鱼。我的意思是，我通常不会和他们交往。

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:25.130
<v Tim>Well, as he puts it, the killer whale versus the guppies, we're all guppies and he's the killer whale--
<v Tim>嗯，正如他所说，虎鲸对阵孔雀鱼，我们都是孔雀鱼，而他是虎鲸——

00:13:25.130 --> 00:13:30.630
<v Jack Hitt>That's Tim, who plays Osric. And as a minnow/guppy, his take is a little different.
<v Jack Hitt>那是扮演奥斯里克的蒂姆。作为一个小鱼/孔雀鱼，他的看法略有不同。

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:49.770
<v Tim>In dealing with an individual like a killer whale, a lot of it-- a lot of, even for him, is a lot of show. The guys see him out in the yard, all right. And now some of the guys that have come into Hamlet, seeing how he is in acting and the seriousness that he takes, he's not what his persona is.
<v Tim>在与像虎鲸这样的人打交道时，很多——对他来说，很多也是表演。大家在院子里看到他，好的。现在一些加入《哈姆雷特》的人看到他在表演中的样子以及他对待这件事的认真程度，他并不是那种他平时表现出来的样子。

00:13:49.770 --> 00:14:00.390
<v Jack Hitt>In other words, he's an actor. But Big Hutch is also a critic. His criticism is sharp and extends to places most actors avoid-- his own character, Horatio.
<v Jack Hitt>换句话说，他是个演员。但大哈奇也是个评论家。他的批评非常尖锐，延伸到大多数演员会避免的地方——他自己的角色，霍拉旭。

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:03.990
<v Big Hutch>I think he a chump, for real.
<v Big Hutch>我觉得他是个笨蛋，真的。

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:04.450
<v Jack Hitt>Really?
<v Jack Hitt>真的吗？

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:42.560
<v Big Hutch>Yeah, I think he a chump. I mean, he supposed to be cool with Hamlet. And they're best friends. But I think Horatio is just somebody-- a sounding board for Hamlet. I mean, the majority of his lines is, eh, my lord, yes, my lord. I mean, if we're friends, we're going to communicate better than that. I mean, you're going to tell me your deepest secrets. So I want to know what you and Ophelia did last night.
<v Big Hutch>是啊，我觉得他是个笨蛋。我的意思是，他应该是哈姆雷特的好哥们。他们是最好的朋友。但我觉得霍拉旭只是某个人——哈姆雷特的传声筒。我的意思是，他大部分的台词就是，嗯，大人，是的，大人。我的意思是，如果我们是朋友，我们会沟通得比这更好。我的意思是，你会告诉我你内心最深处的秘密。所以我很想知道你和奥菲莉娅昨晚做了什么。

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:57.240
<v Big Hutch>I believe it. He should have been a little bit-- show me that I'm truly Hamlet's friend. Don't wait till I get to the end of Act V and I'm getting ready to drink a cup of poison and you stop me. You know? Let me know down the line, man, that I'm really your friend, you know?
<v Big Hutch>我相信这一点。他应该稍微——让我感觉到我真的是哈姆雷特的朋友。别等到我到了第五幕末尾，准备喝下一杯毒药时你才阻止我。你知道吗？伙计，在过程中让我知道我真的是你的朋友，你懂吗？

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:18.510
<v Jack Hitt>Have you ever heard anybody talk about Shakespeare's characters this way? Hutch was always doing this-- talking tough, but then betraying a real gift for literary criticism. Call it his inner minnow. In fact, he pointed out a weakness in the structure of the story I'd never heard before in all my experience with the play-- that Hamlet's dilemma over killing Claudius isn't really much of a dilemma.
<v Jack Hitt>你有没有听过别人这样谈论莎士比亚的角色？哈奇总是这样——说话强硬，但随后流露出真正的文学评论天赋。称之为他内心的小鱼。事实上，他指出了我所有关于这出戏的经验中从未听过的故事结构弱点——哈姆雷特对杀死克劳狄斯的纠结其实并不是什么纠结。

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:40.170
<v Big Hutch>I don't see the conflict. I don't see what Hamlet is dealing with, man, that-- oh, I should kill the king now. I shouldn't kill him now. No, you knew once your father said, revenge, you knew you was going to do this. So what's the hubbub about? Do it.
<v Big Hutch>我不明白冲突在哪里。我不明白哈姆雷特在处理什么，伙计，那——哦，我现在应该杀国王吗？我不应该杀他吗？不，你父亲说复仇的时候你就知道你会这么做。那么为什么要纠结呢？做吧。

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:58.050
<v Big Hutch>I mean, that's the same way I couldn't see somebody raping my daughter or something and just sitting around. No, no, no, no, no. I got to do you, man. And that's just-- you done. That's why I think Hamlet is an old minnow too, man. He-- you know.
<v Big Hutch>我的意思是，就像我无法想象有人强奸了我的女儿，而我只是坐在那里。不，不，不，不，不。我必须干掉你，伙计。就是这样——你完了。这就是为什么我觉得哈姆雷特也是一条老小鱼，伙计。他——你知道。

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:13.840
<v Jack Hitt>Are there no reasons to delay taking swift action, even if you're convinced that you've been wronged? I mean, that's why he stages the play within the play, right? To make sure that Claudius is the bad guy.
<v Jack Hitt>难道即便你确信自己受到了冤枉，也没有理由推迟迅速采取行动吗？我的意思是，这就是他为什么要安排“戏中戏”的原因，对吧？为了确保克劳狄斯真的是坏人。

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:31.650
<v Big Hutch>See, that's why we've got to go back. Now, if I'm strong enough to believe in ghosts, you know, I'm strong enough to believe what that ghost tells me. (CHUCKLING) If I'm strong enough to believe you're a ghost, then I'm sure you know what happened to you.
<v Big Hutch>看，这就是我们得回溯的地方。现在，如果我足够强大到相信鬼魂，你知道，我当然足够强大到相信那个鬼魂告诉我的事。（笑声）如果我足够强大到相信你是个鬼魂，那我肯定你知道发生在你身上的一切。

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:56.600
<v Jack Hitt>Once Hutch got on this riff, he kept going. Denmark's a prison, Hamlet tells Rosencrantz, in Act II. And Hutch says you could do a version of the play that takes this central metaphor literally. All the characters in the play are types he sees in the yard every day. The Claudiuses, who will do anything for the emblems of power-- money, drugs, high-end tennis shoes; Poloniuses, who kiss up to the powerful; Rosencrantz and Guildensterns.
<v Jack Hitt>一旦哈奇开始了这个话题，他就停不下来了。“丹麦是一所监狱”，哈姆雷特在第二幕对罗森格兰兹说。哈奇说你可以制作一个从字面上理解这个中心隐喻的版本。剧中所有的角色都是他在院子里每天见到的类型。那些为权力象征——金钱、毒品、高档网球鞋——做任何事的克劳狄斯；那些向权力献媚的波洛尼厄斯；罗森格兰兹和吉尔登斯特恩。

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:08.960
<v Big Hutch>Rosencrantz and Guildenstern. Oh, man. Administration puts some guys out there just to get some info. Yeah, you got your rats.
<v Big Hutch>罗森格兰兹和吉尔登斯特恩。哦，天哪。管理层安插了一些人只是为了获取信息。是的，你懂的，你的告密者。

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:18.480
<v Jack Hitt>And in Hutch's version, he solves the play's structural problem, fabricating a true dilemma for Hamlet by giving him good reasons to kill Claudius and not to kill him.
<v Jack Hitt>在哈奇的版本中，他解决了这出戏的结构问题，通过给哈姆雷特提供杀死或不杀克劳狄斯的充分理由，制造了一个真正的困境。

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:44.090
<v Big Hutch>This is the way it would happen. Claudius killed this guy here because the guy had the biggest dope business in prison, or something. Gertrude would be some sissy. You find them everywhere in prison. But the guy they kill be Hamlet's brother.
<v Big Hutch>事情会是这样发生的。克劳狄斯杀了这个家伙，因为那家伙在监狱里有最大的毒品生意，或者类似的事情。格特鲁德会是个娘娘腔。你在监狱里到处都能找到这种人。但他们杀死的人是哈姆雷特的兄弟。

00:17:44.090 --> 00:18:20.540
<v Big Hutch>So now, being in the prison world, he must defend that honor. But he got a girl talking about, you only got five years. You know, you did two. Do one more, they're gonna parole you. Come on home, because I love you, and I'm duh-duh-duh-duh. He got another brother in business out there that he can get with to help raise his status. So he got-- he got all these things to look forward to on the street.
<v Big Hutch>所以现在，身处监狱世界，他必须捍卫那种荣誉。但他有个女孩一直在说，你只剩五年了。你知道，你已经坐了两年。再坐一年，你就能假释了。回来吧，因为我爱你，还有巴拉巴拉。他外面还有另一个合伙的兄弟，他可以去找他帮忙提升自己的地位。所以他有——他有所有这些在街上期待的东西。

00:18:20.540 --> 00:19:01.550
<v Big Hutch>But if he let that killing go, he'll have the roughest three years of his entire life. He'll be the piss-pot of the institution. So I mean, he got this dilemma. Would he be strong enough to survive that and get out there? Hutch wouldn't. I ain't gonna lie. I-- hey.
<v Big Hutch>但如果他放过那场谋杀，他将度过他一生中最艰难的三年。他将成为监狱里的受气包。所以我的意思是，他陷入了这个困境。他足够强大到挺过这一切并走出去吗？哈奇不会。我不会撒谎。我——嘿。

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:05.760
<v Agnes Wilcox>OK. Places, please, for the top of Act I.
<v Agnes Wilcox>好的。各就各位，请准备开始第一幕。

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:35.320
<v Jack Hitt>Two months after my visit with Hutch at the first read-through, I was cleared once again into the old classroom. The actors tell me they've been practicing their lines wherever they can, often shouting them from cell to cell. Agnes has also had local professors come in and lecture. She assigns the cast essays to write about their characters. And the Hamlets have been walking the track, memorizing their lines, as has Danny, the Gravedigger. This rehearsal, they're already tinkering with tempo and intonation. That first read-through seems like eons ago.
<v Jack Hitt>在我与哈奇进行第一次剧本研读访问后的两个月，我又一次被批准进入那间旧教室。演员们告诉我，他们一直在任何地方练习台词，经常从牢房到牢房大声喊出来。艾格尼丝还请了当地的教授进来讲课。她给演员们布置了关于他们角色的文章。哈姆雷特们一直在轨道上走着，背诵台词，饰演掘墓人的丹尼也是。这次排练，他们已经在调整节奏和语调。那第一次剧本研读似乎已是遥远的往事了。

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:41.920
<v Actor>This same skull, sir, was, sir, Yorick's skull, the king's jester.
<v Actor>这个头骨，先生，确实，先生，是约里克的头骨，那个国王的弄臣。

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:43.110
<v Actor>This?
<v Actor>这个？

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:46.020
<v Actor>Even that.
<v Actor>正是。

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:52.920
<v Actor>Alas, poor Yorick. I knew him, Horatio.
<v Actor>唉，可怜的约里克。我认识他，霍拉旭。

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.770
<v Actor>He hath born me on his back 1,000 times.
<v Actor>他曾一千次把我背在背上。

00:19:55.770 --> 00:20:01.210
<v Actor>And now, how abhorred in my imagination it is. My gorge rises at it.
<v Actor>现在，我的想象中它是多么令人厌恶。我的胃里一阵翻江倒海。

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:09.630
<v Jack Hitt>A lot of what you see looks like any rehearsal. Agnes is taking notes, a couple of guys are reading to themselves, and the occasional line reading devolves into laughter.
<v Jack Hitt>你看到的很多东西看起来就像任何排练一样。艾格尼丝在做笔记，几个家伙在自言自语，偶尔的台词诵读演变成笑声。

00:20:09.630 --> 00:20:14.300
<v Actor>I will fight with him, (CHUCKLING) upon the-- this thing.
<v Actor>我要和他决斗，（笑声）关于——这件事。

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:19.830
<v Jack Hitt>The scene that's really slaying everyone tonight is the first appearance of Osric, the king's toadying courtier.
<v Jack Hitt>今晚真正让每个人都笑翻的场景是奥斯里克的首次登场，国王那个谄媚的廷臣。

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:24.820
<v Timothy Lantz>Sweet lord, if your lordship were at leisure, I should impart a thing to you from his majesty.
<v Timothy Lantz>亲爱的大人，如果您的贵体有暇，我应该向您转达一件来自陛下的事。

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:28.380
<v Agnes Wilcox>No, just talk to Paul. Just talk to Paul.
<v Agnes Wilcox>不，只对保罗说话。只对保罗说话。

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:29.040
<v Timothy Lantz>Just talk to Paul?
<v Timothy Lantz>只对保罗说话？

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:32.400
<v Agnes Wilcox>Yeah. Come on. Let's stay in the scene.
<v Agnes Wilcox>是的。来吧。我们留在场景里。

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:56.460
<v Timothy Lantz>My name is Timothy Lantz. I'm 38 years old, and I play the role of Osric. Osric is what they call a fop, which a lot of people tend to say, well, he's-- it's a sexual gender thing where he's homosexual or whatever, but he's not. What he is is a king groupie, a wannabe.
<v Timothy Lantz>我叫蒂莫西·兰茨。今年38岁，我扮演奥斯里克这个角色。奥斯里克是他们所谓的“花花公子”，很多人倾向于说，嗯，他——这是一种性别的怪癖，他是个同性恋什么的，但他不是。他实际上是一个“国王追星族”，一个想出风头的人。

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:18.250
<v Jack Hitt>Tim is the one member of the cast with screen time, one second of it. He used to live in California. And in a Frankie Avalon/Annette Funicello revival, he was in the background in one fleeting camera pan, hitting a volleyball. Tim once ran a trucking company, and like most of the cast doesn't want to discuss his crime. But he does want to talk about how he researched his character-- in the TV room.
<v Jack Hitt>蒂姆是剧组中唯一有银幕经验的一位，虽然只有一秒钟。他过去住在加利福尼亚。在一部弗兰基·阿瓦隆/安妮特·方尼塞洛的复兴片中，他在背景里闪过的一个镜头中打排球。蒂姆曾经经营一家货运公司，像大多数演员一样，他不想讨论他的罪行。但他确实想谈谈他是如何研究这个角色的——在电视房里。

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:42.930
<v Timothy Lantz>There are so many fops in television and movies. Nathan Lane was a little bit too much. I think Robin Williams was a little too hyper. The one that really stood out was David Hyde Pierce, his character of Niles Crane. I'd watch Frasier and kind of look at how he did things and his mannerisms, and it worked out pretty good.
<v Timothy Lantz>电视和电影里有太多的花花公子了。内森·莱恩那个有点太过了。我觉得罗宾·威廉姆斯那个又太亢奋了。真正突出的一个是戴维·海德·皮尔斯，他饰演的奈尔斯·克雷恩。我会看《欢乐一家亲》，观察他是怎么做动作和他的举止的，效果还不错。

00:21:42.930 --> 00:21:49.110
<v Timothy Lantz>I mean, sir, for his weapon. But in the imputation laid on him by them and his mean, he is unfellowed.
<v Timothy Lantz>我的意思是，先生，为了他的武器。但根据他人的推断，在那种意味上，他是独一无二的。

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:50.880
<v Actor>What's his weapon?
<v Actor>他的武器是什么？

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.970
<v Timothy Lantz>Rapier and dagger.
<v Timothy Lantz>刺剑和匕首。

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:56.300
<v Inmate>What you say?
<v Inmate>你说什么？

00:21:56.300 --> 00:22:08.390
<v Jack Hitt>Do you think your audience is going to be able to draw the very, sort of, fine distinction you're making between a fop and a gay person?
<v Jack Hitt>你认为你的观众能够区分你所做的那种，非常微妙的，花花公子和同性恋者之间的区别吗？

00:22:08.390 --> 00:22:14.270
<v Timothy Lantz>Not really.
<v Timothy Lantz>说实话，不太行。

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:43.150
<v Jack Hitt>To me, this seemed like an especially risky interpretation of the part. But Tim explains that folks on the outside have a TV movie understanding of prison sexuality. There is a small group of gay prisoners in the closet, another smaller group that is actually out, and then there's the vast majority of prisoners-- straight men, not having any sex at all. Just like outside, Tim says.
<v Jack Hitt>对我来说，这似乎是一个特别有风险的表演解读。但蒂姆解释说，外面的大众对监狱里的性倾向有一种电视电影式的理解。监狱里有一小群深柜的同性恋囚犯，另一小群实际上是出柜的，然后是绝大多数的囚犯——直男，根本没有任何性行为。就像在外面一样，蒂姆说。

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:50.900
<v Agnes Wilcox>OK, gentle folk, let's refocus. We're going to start where Laertes leaps into the grave.
<v Agnes Wilcox>好的，各位，重新聚焦。我们从雷欧提斯跳进坟墓的地方开始。

00:22:50.900 --> 00:23:21.920
<v Jack Hitt>Putting on a play in prison is different. First, Agnes always has to deal with some last minute crisis. Tonight, one of the Hamlets can't come. He was assaulted and was placed in solitary for protection. Plus, everyone must be strip-searched in the side room before and after every meeting with Agnes or with me. Worse, it's incredibly hot. The room is stifling. The only relief is an ancient stand-up fan in the corner. And a lot of the time, the actors are just trying to figure out what their lines mean. Here's Chris, one of the Hamlets.
<v Jack Hitt>在监狱里演戏是不一样的。首先，艾格尼丝总是得处理一些最后一刻的危机。今晚，其中一个哈姆雷特没能来。他被袭击了，为了安全被关进了禁闭室。另外，每次和艾格尼丝或我见面之前和之后，每个人都必须在侧室接受搜身。更糟糕的是，天气热得不可思议。房间里闷热难耐。唯一的缓解是角落里那台老旧的立式风扇。而且很多时候，演员们只是在试图弄明白他们的台词是什么意思。这是克里斯，哈姆雷特之一。

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:38.400
<v Big Hutch>There is a piece of dialogue I give in V.ii in which I'm talking to Horatio about how I got Rosencrantz and Guildenstern. And it starts like, "Up from my cabin, sea-gown scarf'd about me."
<v Big Hutch>我在第五幕第二场有一段台词，我正在和霍拉旭谈论我如何对付罗森格兰兹和吉尔登斯特恩。它是这样开头的，“从船舱出来，海服围着我。”

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:43.030
<v Chris Harris>Up from my cabin, my sea-gown scarfed about me.
<v Chris Harris>从船舱出来，我的海服围着我。

00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:50.450
<v Chris Harris>I didn't know what that meant. And one thing Agnes will do, she'll stop us in mid-line. And she says, what does that mean?
<v Chris Harris>我不知道那是什么意思。艾格尼丝会做的一件事是，她会在台词中间打断我们。她说，那是什么意思？

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:58.010
<v Agnes Wilcox>Let's go back. Remember that what you're wrapped in is not a cloak. It's fog. How do you describe it?
<v Agnes Wilcox>我们回去吧。记住，你裹着的不是一件斗篷。是雾。你如何描述它？

00:23:58.010 --> 00:23:59.840
<v Chris Harris>Sea-gown.
<v Chris Harris>海服。

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:00.820
<v Agnes Wilcox>Your sea-gown.
<v Agnes Wilcox>你的海服。

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:01.980
<v Chris Harris>Scarfed about me.
<v Chris Harris>围着我。

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:04.760
<v Agnes Wilcox>Yeah. Your sea-gown scarfed about you is the fog.
<v Agnes Wilcox>是的。你的海服围着你就是那层雾。

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:05.680
<v Chris Harris>I knew that.
<v Chris Harris>我知道那个。

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:09.080
<v Agnes Wilcox>Yeah. But now you've got to act it.
<v Agnes Wilcox>是的。但现在你必须表演出来。

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:40.510
<v Chris Harris>The sea-gown scarfed about me is the fog. I'm out at night. And it's the flow of the words. Up from my cabin, sea-gown scarfed about me, groped I in the dark to find out them. Shakespeare really put some work in this-- and this is the only play I've really studied from him. But he really is good.
<v Chris Harris>围着我的海服就是雾。我在深夜。这是词语的流动。从船舱出来，海服围着我，我在黑暗中摸索着找到他们。莎士比亚确实在这上面下了一番功夫——这是我真正研究过的他唯一的剧作。但他确实很棒。

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:41.990
<v Agnes Wilcox>OK. Pick up the pace, y'all.
<v Agnes Wilcox>好的。加快节奏，各位。

