00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:29.430
<v Jack Hitt>The cast is such a mix. There's Chris, who's a member of Toastmasters International, and when not rehearsing likes to listen to Journey on his Walkman, while his good pal Brat, a former gang-banger, has a subscription to a lapidary magazine. Then there's Edgar, a former Post Office employee, and Mike, a fight coordinator and a devout Wiccan. And then Stan, who's obviously a college educated businessman, now trying his hand at writing Zane Grey Westerns. And of course, there's Hutch, the killer whale.
<v Jack Hitt>剧组里的人混杂得很有趣。有克里斯，他是国际演讲会的成员，不排练时喜欢在随身听里听Journey乐队的歌；他的好朋友布拉特，一个前黑帮成员，订阅了一本宝石加工杂志。还有埃德加，一个前邮局员工；迈克，一名格斗协调员，也是虔诚的威卡教徒。还有斯坦，显然是一个受过高等教育的商人，现在试着写赞恩·格雷式的西部小说。当然，还有大哈奇，那条虎鲸。

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:35.450
<v Jack Hitt>What keeps them coming back to D-168, where Agnes, this tiny, tough lady, bosses them around? Here's Edgar.
<v Jack Hitt>是什么让他们回到D-168室，在那里接受艾格尼丝这位矮小、强硬的女士指挥？这是埃德加。

00:25:35.450 --> 00:26:13.190
<v Edgar Evans>She makes us feel human, man. She really does. When I go in there, I have to take my clothes off and get butt naked and bend over and spread my cheeks so some man can look up my butt, you know, all the dehuman-- the humiliating things that they do to us here. And when she comes in and does what she does, for that minute, them two and a half hours, all these guys with PhDs and can be doing other things, they come in, I at least can feel human in here.
<v Edgar Evans>她让我们感觉自己是个人，伙计。她确实做到了。当我进去时，我得脱光衣服，弯下腰，分开屁股，好让男人检查我的臀部，你知道，所有那些他们在这里对我们做的非人——屈辱的事情。而当她进来做她所做的时，在那一分钟，那两个半小时里，那些有博士学位可以做其他事情的家伙们进来，我至少在这里能感觉自己是个人。

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:21.890
<v Brat Jones>I think this is taking me to being sane. For just one day, just one day I'm sane enough, you know?
<v Brat Jones>我觉得这让我保持清醒。就为了这一天，就为了这一天，我足够清醒，你知道吗？

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:24.120
<v Jack Hitt>This is Brat Jones, another one of the Hamlets.
<v Jack Hitt>这是布拉特·琼斯，另一位哈姆雷特。

00:26:24.120 --> 00:27:04.050
<v Brat Jones>If you don't keep exercising your mind, then you start to lose it, right? You know, that's possible, right? This gives me an opportunity to see a society beyond what I'm used to. I'm familiar with rap music and videos and big butts on the TV and all that. But let me come back to something that I'm not familiar with. You know, let me get into something else. You know, that did open my eyes into getting into reading Sylvia Plath and Frost and Wadsworth and different other people.
<v Brat Jones>如果你不继续锻炼你的大脑，那你就会开始失去它，对吧？你知道，这很有可能，对吧？这给了我一个机会去看看我习惯之外的社会。我熟悉说唱音乐和录像带，电视里的大屁股等等。但让我回到我不熟悉的东西上来。你知道，让我投入到别的东西里去。你知道，这确实打开了我的眼界，让我开始阅读西尔维娅·普拉斯、弗罗斯特、华兹华斯和其他人的作品。

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:23.820
<v Jack Hitt>Everybody had powerful answers for why they were in the play. One guy with a third grade education level said that he was surprised to find out that he wasn't stupid, just uneducated. And for almost all of them, acting was beside the point. But one inmate got bit by the drama bug, and bit hard. His first step onto the boards was a revelation.
<v Jack Hitt>每个人都有关于为什么参与这部戏的有力回答。一个只有三年级文化水平的家伙说，他惊讶地发现自己并不笨，只是没受过教育。对他们大多数人来说，表演本身是次要的。但有一个囚犯被戏剧虫咬了一口，而且咬得很深。他踏上舞台的第一步就是一场启示。

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:44.170
<v James Word>I'm James Word. I'm 32 years old. And I play the role of Laertes. My wife told me that I should be an actor. And she's like, you can act. You should be an actor. She's had the opportunity to see me deal with certain people on certain levels, and she's seen me change my--
<v James Word>我叫詹姆斯·沃德。今年32岁。我扮演雷欧提斯这个角色。我妻子告诉我，我应该当个演员。她说，你能演戏。你应该当个演员。她有机会看到我在某些层面上应对某些人，她看到了我的改变——

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:53.550
<v Jack Hitt>Wait. Certain people on certain levels. OK, come on. Let's-- as we say in the editorial business, let's air that out a little bit.
<v Jack Hitt>等等。某些层面上应对某些人。好的，来吧。正如我们在编辑行业所说，让我们把话说明白一点。

00:27:53.550 --> 00:28:14.060
<v James Word>Well, I've always wanted to be a con. And I've always wanted people to like me. I wanted to be liked. And the environment that I was in, people liked the bad guy. You know, those were the heroes in our neighborhoods, the bad guy.
<v James Word>嗯，我一直想当个罪犯。而且我一直想让别人喜欢我。我想被人喜欢。而在我所处的环境中，人们喜欢坏蛋。你知道，那些是我们街区里的英雄，坏蛋。

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:34.790
<v James Word>And so I went from this real quiet church guy to this real bad guy. You know, and I'd get around them, and I'd be like, yeah, I'm this and that, and would act it-- you know what I'm saying? Act big, bad, tough, and I don't care, when that really wasn't me.
<v James Word>所以我从一个真正安静的去教堂的家伙变成了一个真正的坏蛋。你知道，我会围绕在他们身边，我会表现得——你知道我在说什么吗？装得很强硬、很坏，我不在乎，而那根本不是我。

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:54.030
<v James Word>Because when I went home at night, I felt bad about what I did, and most of the time was scared to death doing what I was doing. And my wife saw that. And I think that's one of the reasons why she's like, you can be an actor.
<v James Word>因为当我晚上回家时，我对我的所作所为感到难过，而且大多数时候在做那些事的时候，我害怕得要死。我妻子看到了这一点。我想这就是她觉得“你可以当个演员”的原因之一。

00:28:54.030 --> 00:29:07.650
<v Jack Hitt>James Word looks like an actor. He's young and handsome with a smile that can carry him through just about any situation. He plays Laertes, the brother of Ophelia. For the first few acts, Laertes was played by an Amish pedophile. That's another story.
<v Jack Hitt>詹姆斯·沃德看起来就像个演员。他年轻帅气，笑容可以让他应对任何情况。他扮演奥菲莉娅的哥哥雷欧提斯。前几幕中，雷欧提斯是由一个阿米什恋童癖扮演的。那是另一个故事。

00:29:07.650 --> 00:29:25.440
<v Jack Hitt>But the Amish pedophile got transferred, and so it's almost legend in the prison now, like something out of 42nd Street. James Word stepped into the role. He created a sensation among the cast when they realized what a really forceful performance could do for a character. Word made a lasting impression, even on the killer whale.
<v Jack Hitt>但那个阿米什恋童癖被转狱了，所以现在在监狱里这几乎成了传说，就像《第42街》里的情节一样。詹姆斯·沃德接替了这个角色。当剧组意识到一场真正强有力的表演能为一个角色带来什么时，他引起了轰动。沃德留下了深刻的印象，甚至连那条虎鲸都被打动了。

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:45.170
<v Big Hutch>You come along and in Act IV, and you get this guy, James Word. And Word's playing Laertes. And he was hittin' it, especially when he was mourning the death of Ophelia. Is it Scene 4 or something?
<v Big Hutch>你出现了，在第四幕，你得到了这个家伙，詹姆斯·沃德。沃德扮演雷欧提斯。他演得很棒，特别是在他哀悼奥菲莉娅之死的时候。是第四场还是什么？

00:29:45.170 --> 00:30:04.830
<v Big Hutch>He makes his entrance. And he's going to be after the king and the queen. Stop them. Hold up. And man, you should have seen this guy Word man. He come in there like-- dropped to his knees. And he did, why! Why! I said, man, that should have been my part there.
<v Big Hutch>他登场了。他要去找国王和王后。阻止他们。停下。伙计，你应该看看这个叫沃德的家伙。他走进来，就像——跪在地上。他说，为什么！为什么！我说，伙计，那部分本该是我的角色。

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:30.750
<v James Word>I knew I had done a good job. You know, I knew I had done a good job. But when it was over, you know, and everybody was leaving and shaking hands and interacting with each other, the comments that was coming afterwards-- you know, we had our questions and answers. So many people was just-- man, you were so good. Have you done this before? You should do this more. You should continue this.
<v James Word>我知道我做得很好。你知道，我知道我做得很好。但当演出结束，每个人都离开，握手，相互交流时，随之而来的评价——你知道，我们有问答环节。很多人只是说——伙计，你太棒了。你以前做过这个吗？你应该多做这个。你应该继续下去。

00:30:30.750 --> 00:31:10.660
<v James Word>And that feeling for me was just-- it was one of the best feelings I've ever felt. It was like the day my daughter was born. And it made me want to be better. Not just in acting. I mean, it just opened up a whole world for me, you know? Like, man, if I apply myself, I can pretty much do whatever I want.
<v James Word>那种感觉对我来说只是——那是我感受过的最好的感觉之一。就像我女儿出生的那天一样。这让我想变得更好。不仅是在表演上。我的意思是，它为我打开了一个全新的世界，你知道吗？就像，伙计，如果我付出努力，我几乎可以做任何我想做的事。

00:31:10.660 --> 00:31:29.650
<v James Word>O treble woe, fall 10 times treble, on that cursed head, whose wicked deed that most ingenious sense deprived thee of. Now, pile your dust upon the quick and dead, till of this flat a mountain you have made to overtop old Pelion, or the skyish head of blue Olympus.
<v James Word>哦，三重痛苦，降临十倍于我，在那个被诅咒的人头上，他的邪恶行为剥夺了你那聪慧的感觉。现在，把尘土堆在生者和死者身上，直到你把这一平地堆成一座山，高过古老的皮立翁山，或者高耸入云的奥林匹斯山。

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:51.400
<v Jack Hitt>The first chance I get to see Word in action is at tonight's rehearsal, where Laertes goes to the grave of his sister Ophelia. Word was a natural, and talked about acting that way too. Laertes' emotions in the final act roller-coaster from grief to fury to shameful regret. To pull it off, Word channels Laertes' character in a way that should make any method actor cringe with jealousy.
<v Jack Hitt>我见到沃德表演的第一次机会是今晚的排练，雷欧提斯前往他妹妹奥菲莉娅的坟墓。沃德是天生的演员，谈论表演的方式也一样。雷欧提斯在最后一幕的情绪在悲伤、愤怒和羞耻的悔恨中过山车般起伏。为了完美呈现，沃德以一种会让任何方法派演员因嫉妒而畏缩的方式引导着雷欧提斯这个角色。

00:31:51.400 --> 00:32:01.170
<v James Word>Coming into Act IV, he was very angry, violently angry. And I can identify with that, and I can play that role very well, because I've been playing that role all my life.
<v James Word>进入第四幕时，他非常愤怒，暴怒。我可以体会那种情绪，我可以演好那个角色，因为我一生都在演那个角色。

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:03.070
<v James Word>Must there no more be done?
<v James Word>难道没有更多的事要做了吗？

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:04.660
<v Actor>No more be done.
<v Actor>没有更多的事要做了。

00:32:04.660 --> 00:32:15.220
<v James Word>Lay her in the earth, and from her fair and unpolluted flesh may violets spring. I tell thee, old churlish priest, a ministering angel shall my sister be when thou liest howling.
<v James Word>把她放进土里，从她那纯洁无瑕的肉体里可能会长出紫罗兰。我告诉你，这个粗鲁的老牧师，当你在下面哀嚎的时候，我妹妹将成为一个侍奉的天使。

00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:39.480
<v James Word>You know what I'm saying? And Laertes, he falls into the manipulation. And he becomes a bad guy for a little while because he's being deceitful now. You know, I never really looked at it, and it's somewhat cowardly. And I can relate that to my past life as a criminal.
<v James Word>你知道我在说什么吗？雷欧提斯，他陷入了操纵之中。他变成了一个坏蛋，有一会儿，因为他现在变得虚伪了。你知道，我以前从来没这样看过，而且这有点懦弱。我可以把它和我的犯罪过去联系起来。

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:53.590
<v James Word>To put a gun in somebody's face, that's an unfair advantage. You know, and that's a cowardly act. That's what criminals are. We're cowards. You know, when we're criminals, we are cowards.
<v James Word>把枪顶在别人的脸上，那是不公平的优势。你知道，那是一种懦弱的行为。这就是罪犯的本质。我们是懦夫。你知道，当我们成为罪犯时，我们就是懦夫。

00:32:53.590 --> 00:33:04.140
<v Jack Hitt>Do you feel like you can be Laertes because so much of Laertes is inside James Word?
<v Jack Hitt>你觉得你能演好雷欧提斯，是因为雷欧提斯有很大一部分就在詹姆斯·沃德体内吗？

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:17.650
<v James Word>I am Laertes. I am. I am.
<v James Word>我就是雷欧提斯。我是。我就是。

00:33:17.650 --> 00:33:29.220
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, what the ghost of Hamlet's father can teach you about murder. In a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，哈姆雷特的父亲的鬼魂能教你关于谋杀的什么道理。稍后回来，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:47.610
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. If you're just tuning in, what we're doing today is that we're devoting our whole show to Jack Hitt's story, from the Missouri Eastern Correctional Center, where over the course of six months Jack watched prisoners rehearse and stage Act V of Hamlet. Again, here's Jack.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。如果你刚调过来，我们今天做的就是把整期节目贡献给杰克·希特关于密苏里州东部惩教中心的故事，在那里，杰克在六个月的时间里观察囚犯们排练并上演了《哈姆雷特》第五幕。再次欢迎杰克。

00:33:47.610 --> 00:34:13.020
<v Jack Hitt>As the performance day grew closer and I spoke to more and more of the actors, it became clear that each actor used his past, in dense psychological ways, to understand his part. All the Hamlets saw themselves in their version of the Dane, and Edgar would stop me in the hall to tell me that he thought evil King Claudius had some redeeming qualities. But for one actor, the relationship was even more complicated. He used his part to help understand his past.
<v Jack Hitt>随着演出日的临近，我与越来越多的演员交谈，很明显，每个演员都在以深刻的心理方式利用自己的过去来理解他们的角色。所有的哈姆雷特都在这个丹麦王子身上看到了自己，埃德加会在走廊拦住我，告诉我他认为邪恶的克劳狄斯国王有一些可取之处。但对于一名演员来说，这种关系甚至更加复杂。他利用自己的角色来帮助理解他的过去。

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:41.520
<v Danny Waller>My name is Danny Waller. I'm 44 years old. The character that I've played was the ghost of Hamlet's father. The reason I chose that, when I first read the script, the words jumped out at me. And they made me feel things that I haven't felt before.
<v Danny Waller>我叫丹尼·沃勒。今年44岁。我扮演的角色是哈姆雷特的父亲的鬼魂。我选择这个的原因是，当我第一次读剧本时，这些台词触动了我。它们让我感受到了以前从未有过的感觉。

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:50.380
<v Jack Hitt>What in your-- what in your experience drew you to those particular words?
<v Jack Hitt>你自己的——你的经历中是什么吸引你选择了那些特定的台词？

00:34:50.380 --> 00:35:10.160
<v Danny Waller>I took a man's life. And, uh, I felt he was talking to me through that-- that he wanted me to know what I put him through.
<v Danny Waller>我夺走了一个人的生命。而且，呃，我觉得他正在通过那个——那个在和我说话，他想让我知道我让他经历了什么。

00:35:10.160 --> 00:35:39.820
<v Danny Waller>"I am thy father's spirit, doomed for a certain term to walk the night, and for the day confined to fast in fires, 'til the foul crimes done in my days of nature are burnt and purged away. But that I am forbid to tell the secrets of my prison-house, I could a tale unfold, whose lightest words would harrow up thy soul, freeze thy young blood, and make thy two eyes, like stars, start from their spheres."
<v Danny Waller>“我是你父亲的灵魂，注定在夜晚徘徊，白天则被禁锢在火焰中忍受饥渴，直到我在世间所犯下的卑劣罪行被烧灼、净化。如果不是被禁止说出我那监狱里的秘密，我能讲述一个故事，它最轻微的只言片语也会让你灵魂战栗，冻结你年轻的血液，让你那两只眼睛像星星一样脱离眼眶。”

00:35:39.820 --> 00:36:18.100
<v Danny Waller>There's one other spot that goes like this. "Thus was I, sleeping, by a brother's hand of life, of crown, of queen, at once dispatched, cut off even in the blossoms of my sin, no reckoning made, but sent to my account with all my imperfections on my head." And it was pretty much the same way with him. He was taken before his time.
<v Danny Waller>还有一个地方是这样的。“就这样，我在睡眠中被兄弟之手夺走了生命、王冠、王后，一次性被消灭，死在了罪孽的花苞中，没有经过审判，带着我头顶上所有的不完美被送去了结算。”他也是这样。他在他本该离世的时间之前被带走了。

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:30.130
<v Jack Hitt>So when you read the character, do you feel like-- who is talking when you say those lines?
<v Jack Hitt>所以当你读这个角色时，你是否觉得——当你念这些台词时，是谁在说话？

00:36:30.130 --> 00:37:05.920
<v Danny Waller>I'm the body up there. But the words are coming from mostly, uh-- William Pride, the man that I killed. He's mostly the one talking.
<v Danny Waller>我在台上作为躯体。但这些话主要来自——威廉·普莱德，那个被我杀掉的人。主要是他在说话。

00:37:05.920 --> 00:37:23.150
<v Agnes Wilcox>There's not much defense between the actor and the word as you get with an actor who puts his or her training between the two. These guys call it like they see it. And it's true. It's just dead true.
<v Agnes Wilcox>这些家伙不像那些在演员和剧本之间放置了表演训练的演员，他们和台词之间没什么隔阂。他们有话直说。而且这是事实，是极其残酷的真相。

00:37:23.150 --> 00:38:06.470
<v Jack Hitt>That's Agnes, the director. Besides putting on plays in the Missouri Eastern Correctional Center visiting room, she has mounted shows on more familiar stages-- the acclaimed Actor's Studio in New York, the Berkshire Festival in Stockbridge, and the Eugene O'Neill Theater Center in Connecticut. After becoming the artistic director of the New Theater in St. Louis, she started an outreach program to take professional actors doing dramatic works by writers like Don DeLillo and perform them before prison audiences. In time, the prisoners became the actors. And in more time, this side project took over her entire theater company. Now this is what she does-- directing prisoners, who may not have the chops of professional actors but have an intimacy with the material that doesn't exist anywhere else.
<v Jack Hitt>那是艾格尼丝，导演。除了在密苏里州东部惩教中心的探视室排演戏剧外，她还在更熟悉的舞台上执导过——纽约著名的演员工作室、斯托克布里奇的伯克希尔艺术节和康涅狄格州的尤金·奥尼尔戏剧中心。在成为圣路易斯新剧团的艺术总监后，她发起了一个外展项目，让专业演员表演像唐·德里罗这样作家的戏剧作品给囚犯观众看。随着时间的推移，囚犯们成了演员。又过了些时间，这个副业接管了她整个剧团。现在，这就是她的工作——指导囚犯，他们可能没有专业演员的底子，但对剧本有着世界上任何其他地方都不存在的亲密感。

00:38:06.470 --> 00:38:58.120
<v Agnes Wilcox>When Claudius is in the chapel and speaks about his sin and his regret and his ability to undo it, it broke my heart. Because the man playing it felt all of those things fully. And you know, I know these guys have deep regrets. But it was palpable. The audience was stunned. You could hear a pin drop. And that was especially true with the inmate audience. He says, oh, my offense is rank. It smells to heaven. It hath the primal, eldest, curse upon it. A brother's murder.
<v Agnes Wilcox>当克劳狄斯在礼拜堂谈论他的罪恶、遗憾以及他无力扭转这一切时，我的心碎了。因为饰演他的那个人充分感受到了所有这些。你知道，我知道这些家伙有深重的悔恨。但这非常触动人心。观众惊呆了。你可以听到针掉在地上的声音。对于囚犯观众来说尤其如此。他说，哦，我的罪行是如此严重。它发出恶臭直到天堂。它有着最原始、最古老的诅咒。兄弟谋杀。

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:19.890
<v Edgar>A brother's murder. Pray can I not, though inclination be sharp as will. My stronger guilt defeats my strong intent. What if this cursed hand were thicker than itself with brother's blood? Is there not rain enough in the sweet heavens to wash it white as snow?
<v Edgar>兄弟谋杀。祈祷我做不到，尽管意图像意志一样尖锐。我更强烈的负罪感挫败了我强烈的意图。如果这只被诅咒的手沾满了兄弟的鲜血，厚得不能再厚了，怎么办？难道甜美的天堂里没有足够的雨水把它洗得像雪一样白吗？

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:27.940
<v Edgar Evans>My name is Edgar Evans. And I'm 39 years old. And I play the king, King Claudius, in Hamlet.
<v Edgar Evans>我叫埃德加·埃文斯。今年39岁。我在《哈姆雷特》中扮演国王，克劳狄斯国王。

00:39:27.940 --> 00:39:35.750
<v Edgar>And like a man to double business bound, I stand in pause where I shall first begin and both neglect.
<v Edgar>就像一个被双重责任束缚的人，我停在原处，不知道从哪里开始，两边都忽略了。

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:54.950
<v Edgar Evans>I don't consider myself no great actor or nothing, but I try to do the best I can. And when I did the speech, I was looking upward. The chapel was at an incline there. And I was just looking up toward the top, and it was like no one was there but me.
<v Edgar Evans>我不认为自己是什么伟大的演员，但我尽我所能。当我演这段演讲时，我望着上面。礼拜堂在那里有一个倾斜的角度。我只是向上望着顶端，感觉那里除了我之外空无一人。

00:39:54.950 --> 00:40:10.990
<v Edgar Evans>I literally-- I honestly didn't see a soul in the chapel when I was saying this. Maybe even-- and I'm just-- I'm not saying this. It seemed almost like I was praying this actual speech to God.
<v Edgar Evans>我真的——我诚实地说，当我说这段话时，礼拜堂里我一个人也没看到。甚至可能——我不是在说大话。这看起来几乎就像我在向神祈祷这段演讲。

00:40:10.990 --> 00:40:33.180
<v Edgar>But O, what form of prayer can serve my turn. Forgive me my foul murder? That cannot be, since I am still possessed of those effects for which I did the murder-- my crown, my own ambition, and my queen.
<v Edgar>但哦，什么形式的祈祷能拯救我呢？原谅我那卑劣的谋杀？那不可能，因为我仍然占有着我因谋杀而得到的那些果实——我的王冠、我自己的野心，还有我的王后。

00:40:33.180 --> 00:41:02.580
<v Edgar Evans>I have a wife and four kids. And by being incarcerated, I feel that I've really let them down. When I said that speech and my wife was here in the visiting room, I don't know if it had an impact on her. I don't even know if she truly understood all of the content. But I wanted her to hear that speech more than anybody.
<v Edgar Evans>我有妻子和四个孩子。因为被监禁，我觉得我真的辜负了他们。当我说那段话时，我妻子就在探视室里，我不知道这对她是否有影响。我甚至不知道她是否真正理解所有的内容。但我比任何人都想让她听到那段演讲。

00:41:02.580 --> 00:41:39.480
<v Edgar>O, wretched state, O, bosom black as death, O, limed soul, struggling to be free art more engaged. Help, angels. Make assay. Bow, stubborn knee, and hearts with strings of steel, be soft as sinews of the newborn babe. All may be well.
<v Edgar>哦，悲惨的状态，哦，像死一样黑的心，哦，粘了胶的灵魂，挣扎着想自由却反而深陷其中。帮助我，天使们。尝试一下。弯下你倔强的膝盖，那颗用钢弦做成的心，要变得像新生儿的筋骨一样柔软。一切都会好起来的。

00:41:39.480 --> 00:42:00.510
<v Jack Hitt>Edgar wouldn't tell me his crime. Although I found myself playing a constant guessing game with all of them about this, they wouldn't discuss the past. That was then, they said. This is now. But I had to know. So one morning, instead of visiting, I went to downtown St. Louis, not far from the Arch, and sat in a records depository reading old case files.
<v Jack Hitt>埃德加没有告诉我他的罪行。虽然我发现自己一直在和他们所有人玩猜谜游戏，但他们不愿讨论过去。那是过去，他们说。这是现在。但我必须知道。所以一天早上，我不去探访，而是去了市中心，在法院附近，坐在记录保存处读旧的案件档案。

00:42:00.510 --> 00:42:26.160
<v Jack Hitt>It was more horrible than I thought. One guy I particularly liked shot a man in the head twice at point-blank range. Another of my new friends raped his pubescent daughter, impregnating her. Later, there was an abortion. Another friend grabbed a man getting out of a car, put a gun to his chest during a robbery, and pulled the trigger. Others had sodomized children-- younger children, the age of my own children.
<v Jack Hitt>那比我预想的更可怕。我特别喜欢的一个家伙近距离朝一个男人的头上开了两枪。我另一个新朋友强奸了他未成年的女儿，让她怀了孕。后来，做了流产。另一个朋友在抢劫时抓住一个下车的男人，把枪顶在他的胸口，扣动了扳机。其他人则鸡奸了儿童——比我自己的孩子还要小的孩子。

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:45.630
<v Jack Hitt>I didn't fall asleep for a long time in my frigid Ramada Inn room. At 3:00 in the morning, I had one of those cinematic nightmares. I dreamed that a very good friend of mine, an editor at a magazine, had me over to dinner. She got angry with someone else there, pulled out a gun, and plugged him twice, right in the face. Then she asked me not to talk about it with anybody.
<v Jack Hitt>我在冰冷的华美达酒店房间里很长一段时间无法入睡。凌晨三点，我做了一个类似电影场景的噩梦。我梦见我的一个好朋友，一家杂志的编辑，邀请我共进晚餐。她对那里的另一个人发火，掏出一把枪，朝着他的脸开了两枪。然后她要求我不要跟任何人谈论这件事。

00:42:45.630 --> 00:43:19.440
<v Jack Hitt>I panicked. And the next thing I knew, I was sitting up in my hotel bed, panting like a sprinter. It didn't take Freud to figure out what it meant. Someone I knew and liked was a murderer. I wanted to talk to the cast about this, but I was anxious. I know this sounds crazy, but I was afraid it might hurt their feelings. I felt like they had betrayed me. But strangely, I felt that I had betrayed them too. There was only one day left to talk, just before the final performance. So before curtain, I asked Brat to sit down with me. I could barely spit out my question.
<v Jack Hitt>我惊慌失措。下一刻，我发现自己正坐在酒店的床上，像短跑运动员一样气喘吁吁。不用弗洛伊德也能看出这意味着什么。我认识且喜欢的一个人是个杀人犯。我想和剧组谈谈这个，但我很焦虑。我知道这听起来很疯狂，但我担心这可能会伤害他们的感情。我觉得他们背叛了我。但奇怪的是，我觉得我也背叛了他们。只剩下一天时间谈话，就在最后一次演出前。所以，在开场前，我请布拉特坐下来。我几乎吐不出问题来。

00:43:19.440 --> 00:44:05.810
<v Jack Hitt>You know, we came in here three months ago, or five months ago, and met you all for the first time as actors. And so yesterday, we decided to go down to City Hall and read everybody's record. And so we read everybody's record. And I have to say, since we met-- you know, since I met you as an actor first and now I know what you did, it's very hard for me to wrap my mind around this other guy that's on paper. And so I want to know how you do it. How do you square the Brat Jones of-- what was it, 13 years ago-- yeah, yeah-- with the one that I see on the stage tonight.
<v Jack Hitt>你知道，我们三个月前，或者五个月前来到这里，第一次以演员的身份见到你们所有人。昨天，我们决定去市政厅读每个人的记录。所以我们读了所有人的记录。我不得不说，自从我们见面——你知道，自从我先是以演员身份认识你，而现在我知道了你做了什么，要让我的大脑接受纸上的那个“那个人”真的非常困难。所以我想要知道你是怎么做到的。你是如何把13年前的那个布拉特·琼斯——是的，是的——和你今晚在舞台上表现的那个你调和在一起的。

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:32.540
<v Brat Jones>It's taken a lot of practice. I'm trying to discover who I am now. It's not been easy. I was off into the drugs and all those things when I got locked up for that case. And I slowly had to come out of those things. Because I always knew in the back of my mind that I had already went to the lowest point in life. So now it was time to see what I could do as far as going to the highest point in life.
<v Brat Jones>这需要很多练习。我正在努力发掘现在的我是谁。这并不容易。当我因那个案子被关起来时，我沉迷于毒品和所有那些事情。我慢慢地不得不摆脱那些东西。因为我内心深处一直都知道，我已经跌落到了人生的最低点。所以现在是时候看看我在追求人生最高点方面能做些什么了。

00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:49.950
<v Jack Hitt>It was a hard question for any of them to answer. They all said the same thing. But I'm this guy now, I'm not that guy. Are we forever the prisoner of our actions? It's a good question. It was Hamlet's question, and it's the unresolvable conflict in our penal system.
<v Jack Hitt>对于他们中的任何一个来说，这都是个难以回答的问题。他们都说了同样的话。但我现在是这个人，我不是那个人。我们永远是我们行为的囚徒吗？这是一个好问题。这是哈姆雷特的问题，也是我们刑罚制度中无法解决的冲突。

00:44:49.950 --> 00:45:14.720
<v Jack Hitt>Why do we put people in jail? To rehabilitate them and restore them to our company, or to punish them, regardless of how much they might change? One can't hold both these ideas in one's mind simultaneously. That's why our prison debates on TV and in Congress are so vehement and incoherent. The two sides cannot be squared by mere politics. They can't even be squared by the people at the center of the debate. Here's Danny.
<v Jack Hitt>我们为什么要让人坐牢？是为了让他们改过自新并让他们回归社会，还是为了惩罚他们，无论他们发生了多大的变化？一个人不可能同时持有这两种观点。这就是为什么我们在电视和国会上关于监狱的辩论如此激烈且语无伦次的原因。两方不能通过单纯的政治来调和。它们甚至不能被处于辩论中心的人调和。这是丹尼。

00:45:14.720 --> 00:46:12.300
<v Danny Waller>When I first got this sentence, I said, yeah, I'll go ahead and just die in here because I don't deserve to be out there the way I am. You know, that was 13, 14 years ago. But a person changes. I'm no longer the criminal I used to be. I know that I will not do any other crimes out there. But I took a man's life. Do I deserve to be out there? I cannot say.
<v Danny Waller>当我第一次拿到这个判决时，我说，好吧，我就在里面死掉吧，因为我不配在外面像我这样。你知道，那是13、14年前的事了。但人是会变的。我不再是过去那个罪犯了。我知道我在外面不会再犯任何罪。但我夺走了一个人的生命。我配在外面吗？我无法说。

00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:30.020
<v James Word>Hamlet, thou art slain. No medicine in the world can do thee good. In thee is not a half an hour's life. No medicine in the world, blah blah blah. I can't get it. I forgot. Line.
<v James Word>哈姆雷特，你被杀了。世界上没有任何药能救你。你活不到半小时了。世界上没有任何药，吧啦吧啦吧啦。我不记得了。台词。

00:46:30.020 --> 00:46:32.140
<v Agnes Wilcox>The treacherous instrument is in thy hand.
<v Agnes Wilcox>那把致命的凶器就在你手里。

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:51.700
<v Jack Hitt>It's opening night, dress rehearsal. Word has flubbed his line again. Things aren't going well. The only time the authorities would allow a dress rehearsal is in the hour before actual curtain. This is the first time the cast has seen the acting stage, the first time in costume, the first time they have real props, skulls and shovels.
<v Jack Hitt>是首演之夜，彩排。沃德又忘词了。事情进行得不顺利。当局允许彩排的唯一时间是在正式开演前的一小时。这是剧组第一次见到真正的舞台，第一次穿上戏服，第一次拥有真正的道具，头骨和铲子。

00:46:51.700 --> 00:47:03.670
<v Jack Hitt>The shovel is a story in itself, given every prison fear of shovels. You can use them to dig tunnels, remember. The authorities insisted on using only a flimsy cardboard replica. Everyone is tense, even Agnes.
<v Jack Hitt>铲子本身就是一个故事，考虑到监狱里对铲子的恐惧。记住，你可以用它们挖隧道。当局坚持只使用薄薄的纸板模型。每个人都很紧张，甚至艾格尼丝也是。

00:47:03.670 --> 00:47:07.930
<v Agnes Wilcox>Guys, let's double the pace so we can get through this, get it all out. The sight is dismal.
<v Agnes Wilcox>伙计们，我们把节奏加快一倍，这样我们就能熬过去，把这一切都释放出来。这景象真凄凉。

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:11.990
<v Actor>The sight is dismal.
<v Actor>这景象真凄凉。

00:47:11.990 --> 00:47:14.770
<v Agnes Wilcox>And our affairs--
<v Agnes Wilcox>而我们的事务——

00:47:14.770 --> 00:47:22.820
<v Jack Hitt>We are in the prison chapel where their first performance will be held before an audience of inmates. Six months after my first visit, it is, at last, showtime.
<v Jack Hitt>我们就在监狱的礼拜堂里，他们的第一次演出将在囚犯观众面前举行。在我第一次访问六个月后，终于，表演时间到了。

00:47:22.820 --> 00:47:24.730
<v Inmate>Alan, how are you doing today?
<v Inmate>艾伦，你今天怎么样？

00:47:24.730 --> 00:47:32.200
<v Jack Hitt>Armed guards let the audience in the door, one by one. Agnes calls her actors together in a corner and gives them the final pep talk.
<v Jack Hitt>武装警卫一个接一个地让观众进门。艾格尼丝把她的演员们召集在一个角落，给他们最后的鼓励。

00:47:32.200 --> 00:47:59.780
<v Agnes Wilcox>Paul, Edgar, could I have you? OK. Things to think about. Make sure to stand still on yours and other people's punchlines. If something falls apart, pick it up, go somewhere with it. Make up words. I don't think anybody here has memorized it, so we're safe.
<v Agnes Wilcox>保罗，埃德加，能过来一下吗？好的。需要考虑的事情。确保在你的和别人的笑话梗上站定。如果事情乱套了，捡起来，带着它继续。自己编词。我想这里没人完全背下来，所以我们很安全。

00:47:59.780 --> 00:48:15.160
<v Agnes Wilcox>And mostly, this afternoon people were working so hard I don't think you had much fun. Hard work's behind us. This is the night to just have a blast.
<v Agnes Wilcox>最重要的是，今天下午大家工作得太辛苦了，我觉得你们没怎么享受。辛苦工作已经过去了。今晚是尽情玩乐的时刻。

00:48:15.160 --> 00:48:16.760
<v Inmate>Say "One for the team."
<v Inmate>说“为团队贡献一次”。

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:18.120
<v Agnes Wilcox>One for the team.
<v Agnes Wilcox>为团队贡献一次。

00:48:18.120 --> 00:48:23.900
<v Inmate>Ready, set-- hoo-ha!
<v Inmate>预备，开始——呼哈！

00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:57.850
<v Jack Hitt>Once the play starts, Danny and Stan deliver their lines OK, but all that antique Elizabethan wordplay isn't connecting with the audience. But then midway through the scene, the crowd starts to really respond to the emotion in Ophelia's funeral march. Laertes is the last mourner, humming at the side of the grave, tossing rose petals on her corpse. Right away, the action picks up. The Hamlets appear, taunting Laertes about the sincerity of his grief. They quarrel and exchange insults. A duel is proposed, which culminates in the last sword fight scene.
<v Jack Hitt>一旦表演开始，丹尼和斯坦的台词说得还可以，但所有那些古老的伊丽莎白时代的双关语并没有与观众建立联系。但随后场景中途，人群开始真正地对奥菲莉娅葬礼行进中的情绪做出反应。雷欧提斯是最后的送葬者，在坟墓边哼着曲子，把玫瑰花瓣撒在她的尸体上。行动立刻加快了。哈姆雷特们出现了，嘲弄雷欧提斯哀悼的诚意。他们争吵并互相谩骂。一场决斗被提出来，演变成最后一场剑斗场景。

00:48:57.850 --> 00:49:01.250
<v Jack Hitt>[DRUM ROLL]
<v Jack Hitt>[鼓声]

00:49:01.250 --> 00:49:05.140
<v Edgar>Come, begin!
<v Edgar>来，开始吧！

00:49:05.140 --> 00:49:35.810
<v Jack Hitt>Most directors block the scene as a professional fencing match. Agnes has it looking like a knife fight in an alley. The audience totally gets it. Word holds his arms slightly out from his sides, his hand gripping his sword like he would a shiv. He and one of the Hamlets are circling each other, like thugs. Word jerks his shoulders forward-- a pure back alley feint, a fake movement to intimidate his opponent, something all of them have probably seen right in the yard.
<v Jack Hitt>大多数导演把这个场景编排成专业的击剑比赛。艾格尼丝让它看起来像巷子里的械斗。观众完全看懂了。沃德的双臂稍微向两侧伸开，手紧紧握着剑，就像他握着一把自制刀一样。他和其中一个哈姆雷特像暴徒一样互相盘旋。沃德猛地向前耸动肩膀——一个纯粹的小巷假动作，为了威慑对手而做的虚晃，这是他们每个人可能都在院子里见过的东西。

00:49:35.810 --> 00:49:44.380
<v Jack Hitt>[AUDIENCE LAUGHTER]
<v Jack Hitt>[观众笑声]

00:49:44.380 --> 00:49:53.090
<v Jack Hitt>Then the room goes quiet, as the actors starts slicing one another, leading to Laertes' death scene.
<v Jack Hitt>然后房间安静了下来，演员们开始互相割伤，引向雷欧提斯的死亡场景。

00:49:53.090 --> 00:50:41.800
<v James Word>Hamlet, thou art slain. No medicine in the world can do thee good. In thee, there's not half an hour's life. The treacherous instrument is in your hand, unbated and envenomed. The foul practice has turned itself on me. Lo, here I lie, never to rise again. Exchange forgiveness with me, noble Hamlet. Let mine and my father's death be not upon thee.
<v James Word>哈姆雷特，你被杀了。世界上没有任何药能救你。你活不到半小时了。致命的器械在你手里，既没封头又浸了毒。卑劣的诡计已经报应到了我身上。看，我躺在这里，再也站不起来了。和我交换宽恕吧，高贵的哈姆雷特。不要让我和我父亲的死算在你的头上。

00:50:41.800 --> 00:51:04.480
<v Jack Hitt>The audience, like a living thing, leans forward in unison to watch Word slide down the chapel wall and die. There are no cackles of discomfort, no shouts from the gallery. Just breathless silence, the silence of recognition.
<v Jack Hitt>观众们像一个活生生的东西，齐刷刷地前倾，看着沃德顺着礼拜堂的墙壁滑下并死去。没有不舒服的咯咯笑声，没有来自看台的叫喊。只有屏住呼吸的沉默，认同的沉默。

00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:23.020
<v Jack Hitt>In all, there were three performances. And on the final night, before family and dignitaries, the entire cast found the magic that Paul said would happen during that first read-through. The actors knew that this would be the last time they get to perform as a group. So when Danny and Stan came out for their final gravedigger appearance, somehow those Elizabethan jokes worked.
<v Jack Hitt>总共进行了三场演出。在最后一场之夜，在家人和政要面前，全体演员找到了保罗在第一次剧本研读时说的那种魔力。演员们知道这将是他们作为团队表演的最后一次。所以当丹尼和斯坦出来进行他们最后的掘墓人表演时，那些伊丽莎白时代的笑话不知怎么地奏效了。

00:51:23.020 --> 00:51:30.080
<v Actor>Art a heathen? How does thou understand the scripture? The scripture said Adam digged. Could he dig without arms?
<v Actor>你是异教徒吗？你是怎么理解经文的？经文说亚当在挖掘。没有手臂他能挖吗？

00:51:30.080 --> 00:51:31.310
<v Actor>[AUDIENCE CHUCKLING]
<v Actor>[观众笑声]

00:51:31.310 --> 00:52:05.230
<v Jack Hitt>Having seen every performance, I can testify, the actors rose above their talents in that last show. Mike and Hudson as the belligerent priests, Buck and Tommy as the ambassadors, Sylvester's Fortinbras. You could feel the mutual support. Just as missed lines and other mistakes play off one another, and can spiral downward into fiasco, the rhythm of the room can go the other way too. Strong lines beget better performances. The gang of Hamlets came together in a way they hadn't before. That little mob seemed like one voice. But the real surprise for me that last night was Hutch.
<v Jack Hitt>看过每一场演出，我可以证明，演员们在最后一场演出中超越了他们的天赋。迈克和哈德森饰演好战的牧师，巴克和汤米饰演大使，西尔维斯特饰演福丁布拉。你能感受到相互的支持。正如漏掉的台词和其他错误会相互影响，螺旋式下滑成惨败一样，房间的节奏也可以朝另一个方向发展。强有力的台词会带来更好的表演。这群哈姆雷特以一种他们以前没有过的方式团结在一起。那小小的群体似乎成了一个声音。但那天晚上对我来说真正的惊喜是哈奇。

00:52:05.230 --> 00:52:10.500
<v Big Hutch>I am more an antique Roman than a dame. There's still yet some liquor left.
<v Big Hutch>比起女士，我更像是一个古罗马人。这里还有一点酒。

00:52:10.500 --> 00:52:36.590
<v Jack Hitt>It's here that Hutch's Horatio delivers his most famous line. The play is almost over. He's surrounded by corpses and speaks to his dying friend Hamlet. In the other performances, I always thought Hutch had been plagued by what you might call the Jack Nicholson syndrome. The actor's persona is bigger than any role he might play. But tonight, Horatio has Hutch under control and the audience in his hand. He has the one great line to deliver. And as Hutch might say, he nails it.
<v Jack Hitt>就在这里，哈奇饰演的霍拉旭说了他最著名的台词。戏快结束了。他被尸体包围着，对垂死的哈姆雷特说话。在其他演出中，我总觉得哈奇被所谓的“杰克·尼科尔森综合征”所困扰。演员的个人魅力大于他可能扮演的任何角色。但今晚，霍拉旭完全掌控了哈奇，而哈奇也掌控了观众。他有一句伟大的台词要念。正如哈奇所说，他念绝了。

00:52:36.590 --> 00:52:49.200
<v Big Hutch>Now cracks a noble heart. Good night, sweet prince, and flights of angels sing thee to thy rest.
<v Big Hutch>现在，一颗高贵的心碎了。晚安，甜美的王子，愿天使的歌声送你安息。

00:52:49.200 --> 00:52:52.440
<v Big Hutch>[HORNS]
<v Big Hutch>[号角声]

00:52:52.440 --> 00:53:08.070
<v Jack Hitt>So Hamlet dies, and Fortinbras takes over. And then the drums of Hamlet's funeral begin. On other stages, a curtain would fall. But here, beneath the cafeteria's unforgiving fluorescence, the actors just stop. From the back of the room, Agnes says simply--
<v Jack Hitt>哈姆雷特死了，福丁布拉接管了。然后哈姆雷特葬礼的鼓声响起。在其他舞台上，大幕会落下。但在这里，在食堂那无情的荧光灯下，演员们只是停住了。从房间后面，艾格尼丝简单地说——

00:53:08.070 --> 00:53:11.000
<v Agnes Wilcox>End of play.
<v Agnes Wilcox>剧终。

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:22.250
<v Agnes Wilcox>[AUDIENCE APPLAUDING]
<v Agnes Wilcox>[观众掌声]

00:53:22.250 --> 00:53:56.180
<v Jack Hitt>The audience tonight is a mix of St. Louis' artistic elite. It's a theater crowd-- polite, well-dressed people. Many of them have helped fund this production. They want and fully expect to meet the talent afterwards. And for many of the actors, this moment-- they call it the cast party-- is the most prized. James Word and Edgar Evans mentioned it specifically when I talked to them. Just the chance to stand around in a room full of normal people, drink a Coke, and carry on about the play, the future, the weather, the freedom to chat and mingle, like you were in the lobby of a theater instead of a bulletproof visiting room.
<v Jack Hitt>今晚的观众是圣路易斯艺术精英的混合体。这是一个戏剧人群——礼貌、衣着考究的人。他们中的许多人资助了这次演出。他们想要并完全期待之后能见到演员。对许多演员来说，这一刻——他们称之为剧组聚会——是最珍贵的。当我与詹姆斯·沃德和埃德加·埃文斯交谈时，他们特别提到了这一点。只是有机会站在一个充满正常人的房间里，喝杯可乐，聊聊戏、未来、天气、聊天的自由，这就像是在剧院大厅，而不是防弹的探视室。

00:53:56.180 --> 00:53:58.230
<v Inmate>Hey, how you doing?
<v Inmate>嘿，你好吗？

00:53:58.230 --> 00:54:00.120
<v Audience Member>I'm doing well.
<v Audience Member>我很好。

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:01.030
<v Inmate>Good to see you.
<v Inmate>很高兴见到你。

00:54:01.030 --> 00:54:02.050
<v Audience Member>This is my sixth time here.
<v Audience Member>这是我第六次来这儿了。

00:54:02.050 --> 00:54:06.340
<v Inmate>Yeah, you come to all of them. Yeah, I appreciate it. And we thank you. Thank you very much.
<v Inmate>是的，你每次都来。是的，我很感激。我们非常感谢你。非常感谢。

00:54:06.340 --> 00:54:22.300
<v Jack Hitt>In a high-security prison, though, when a play is over, it's over. No sooner had the bows finished than Danny, who doubles as the stage manager, turns right around and starts frantically striking the set. The guards had informed everyone that they'd have about 10 minutes for the cast party.
<v Jack Hitt>在高安全监狱里，然而，当一出戏结束时，它就结束了。鞠躬刚结束，担任舞台经理的丹尼立刻转身，开始疯狂地拆卸布景。警卫通知大家，剧组聚会大约有10分钟时间。

00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:53.890
<v Jack Hitt>Like any play, all the work was for this moment-- to get to the end of the last performance successfully. And now that it's here, these few minutes are shot through with a kind of melancholy. Soon everything will end, and everyone will return to his pre-acting life. Here, that near future is visible. In the side room, everyone sees him, is the guard waiting to strip search them back into the yard. He wears a dull expression on his face and rubber gloves on his hands. But that would be 10 minutes from now. There's still time for pretending.
<v Jack Hitt>像任何演出一样，所有的努力都是为了这一刻——成功地走到最后一场演出的结尾。现在它到了，这短短的几分钟里透着一种忧郁。很快一切都会结束，每个人都会回到他排练前的生活中去。在这里，那种即将到来的未来是清晰可见的。在侧室，每个人都能看到那个守卫，正等着给他们搜身，把他们送回院子里去。他脸上的表情呆滞，手上戴着橡胶手套。但这将是10分钟后的事。现在还有装模作样的时间。

00:54:53.890 --> 00:54:58.270
<v Inmate>Thank you, sir. Thanks for coming out. We appreciate the support.
<v Inmate>谢谢你，先生。谢谢你来观看。我们感谢你们的支持。

00:54:58.270 --> 00:55:00.190
<v Audience Member>I really like this act. I like the--
<v Audience Member>我真的很喜欢这一幕。我喜欢——

00:55:00.190 --> 00:55:01.260
<v Inmate>It was so complicated.
<v Inmate>它太复杂了。

00:55:01.260 --> 00:55:02.440
<v Audience Member>--counter-balance between the beginning and the end.
<v Audience Member>——开始和结尾之间的平衡。

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:31.490
<v Jack Hitt>According to the prison commissioner, 97% of the people locked up today will someday join us on the outside. Manuel is leaving for a halfway house in 48 hours. He could have been out weeks before but chose to stay in prison so he could finish the play. Hutch has a scheduled date for release. And a few more of the cast have parole board hearings coming up to decide whether they've changed enough and should be allowed to mingle with us on the outside. To that extent, this whole night, including the cast party, is just another rehearsal.
<v Jack Hitt>据监狱专员称，今天被关押的人中有97%的人将来会加入我们的社会。曼努埃尔将在48小时内离开监狱前往中途之家。他本可以在几周前就出去了，但选择留在监狱，以便完成这部戏。哈奇有预定的释放日期。还有几位演员有假释委员会的听证会，决定他们是否已经改变了足够多，应该被允许与我们这些外人来往。在某种程度上，整个夜晚，包括剧组聚会，都只是又一场排练。

00:55:31.490 --> 00:55:33.640
<v Inmate>Thanks for coming!
<v Inmate>谢谢你们的到来！

00:55:33.640 --> 00:55:34.630
<v Audience Member>Oh, you were great.
<v Audience Member>哦，你们太棒了。

00:55:34.630 --> 00:55:36.100
<v Inmate>Thank you. I'm glad you enjoyed it.
<v Inmate>谢谢。很高兴你们喜欢。

00:55:36.100 --> 00:55:39.050
<v Audience Member>So, you think you like performing?
<v Audience Member>所以，你觉得你喜欢表演吗？

00:55:39.050 --> 00:55:41.510
<v Inmate>Yes, ma'am.
<v Inmate>是的，女士。

00:55:41.510 --> 00:55:47.430
<v Audience Member>Well, you're very good.
<v Audience Member>嗯，你演得很好。

00:55:47.430 --> 00:55:50.900
<v Ira Glass>Jack Hitt, he's a longtime contributor to our show and a writer for the New York Times Magazine.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·希特，他是我们节目的长期贡献者，也是《纽约时报杂志》的撰稿人。

00:55:50.900 --> 00:56:15.780
<v Ira Glass>Since we first aired this program back in 2002, all of the main actors that Jack to in his story have been released from prison. Edgar, who played Claudius, went to work at a hospital - became chief union steward there - and recently retired. Big Hutch works in public transit. Tim moved to Hollywood hoping to produce his own screenplays. Danny, the guy who talked about speaking as the man he killed -- was released from prison just a few months ago, in May. Manuel got out long ago and died in a truck crash in 2008.
<v Ira Glass>自从我们2002年首次播出这期节目以来，杰克报道故事中的所有主要演员都已出狱。饰演克劳狄斯的埃德加去了一家医院工作，成为那里的工会主管，最近退休了。大哈奇在公共交通部门工作。蒂姆搬到了好莱坞，希望能制作自己的剧本。丹尼，那个谈论着以他杀掉的人的角度说话的家伙——几个月前，即五月份刚出狱。曼努埃尔很久以前就出去了，但在2008年死于一场卡车事故。

00:56:15.780 --> 00:56:31.780
<v Ira Glass>Agnes Wilcox their director died in 2017 after decades of running Prison Performing Arts, which she founded. Chris, Edgar, and Big Hutch were at her funeral. The organization is still going strong. Chris Edgar and Brat are involved with their alumni theater program. You can learn more about them at prisonperformingarts.org.
<v Ira Glass>他们的导演艾格尼丝·威尔科克斯在经营了她创立的“监狱表演艺术”几十年后，于2017年去世。克里斯、埃德加和大哈奇参加了她的葬礼。该组织仍在强劲发展。克里斯、埃德加和布拉特参与了他们的校友戏剧项目。你可以在prisonperformingarts.org了解更多关于他们的信息。

00:56:31.780 --> 00:57:43.590
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "DESPERADO"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - "亡命之徒"]

00:57:43.590 --> 00:57:56.110
<v Ira Glass>This episode of our program was produced by Alex Blumberg, with Starlee Kine, Wendy Dorr, Jonathan Goldstein, and me. Our senior producer for this episode was Julie Snyder. Our technical director for our show is Matt Tierney. Production help with today's show from Diane Wu.
<v Ira Glass>本期节目由亚历克斯·布伦伯格制作，参与人员包括斯塔莉·基恩、温迪·多尔、乔纳森·戈尔茨坦，还有我。本期节目的资深制作人是朱莉·斯奈德。我们节目的技术总监是马特·蒂尔尼。今天节目的制作帮助来自黛安·吴。

00:57:56.110 --> 00:58:04.770
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

00:58:04.770 --> 00:58:18.650
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our programs co-founder Mr. Torey Malatia, or as we like to call him, Shamu.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由公共广播交换平台PRX传送给公共广播电台。像往常一样，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托里·马拉蒂亚先生，或者我们喜欢称呼他为“夏穆”。

00:58:18.650 --> 00:58:23.980
<v Big Hutch>I control the killer whales. And I can eat up them minnows if I want.
<v Big Hutch>我控制虎鲸。如果我想，我可以吃掉小鱼。

00:58:23.980 --> 00:58:28.980
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周带回更多《美国生活》的故事。
